A||N||3||stimpy||Speedometer gremlinz||||13||
Z||000000||stimpy||02-02-2000||10:54 PM||||<BR>This morning while driving to work, I was punishing my stereo system when the radio fuse in the fuse box blew.  I left the office at lunch to buy more fuses and noticed that at this point my speedo was not working. I went to O'Reillys and they could not find a speedo cable in the computer system. Or in their catalogues. Or at their suppliers warehouse. I thanked them for their time and went out into the parking lot to change my fuse, started the car and lo and behold, my speedometer had come back. Is the Fiero's speedo electrically driven? Why did the rest of my guages work?  ||||reg||13
Z||000001||batboy||02-02-2000||11:33 PM||||Yes, the Fiero speedo is electric and has no mechanical cable. The other gauges worked because they're on different circuits.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 02-02-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||02-03-2000||08:35 AM||||This is something I don't mention often, but if you would have watched your odometer while the radio fuse was bad/blown, it too would not be working. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 02-03-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||PAUL88GT||02-03-2000||06:21 PM||||I am having problems with the speedometer in my 87 Trans AM. Could it be electric also? I did notice a wire when working on the starter that looked corroded and weak. My speedometer is jumping and the odometer works off and on. I know this is not a Trans Am site, but I cannot find a good one.  TIA.||||reg||1
